3.text:
For a profile write $a_{i}$ for the number of first choices of $i$ and
after a re-labelling $a_{1}\le a_{2}\le a_{3}\le a_{4}$, $\sum a_{i}=n$
and for $O\ne\varnothing$

\begin{equation*}
c_{i}(O)=\#\{v:i=\max _{O}v\},\qquad 
d_{j}(O)=\#\{v:j\succ _{v}i\;\forall i\in O\},\qquad j\notin O ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\sum_{i\in O}c_{i}(O)=n$, $c_{i}(O)\ge a_{i}$ on $i\in O$ and

\begin{equation*}
O\ {\rm is}\ t\!-\!u\ {\rm suitable}\iff 
c_{i}(O)>t\ i\in O,\qquad 
\max_{j\notin O}d_{j}(O)\le u .
\end{equation*}
For a triple $O_{w}=[4]\setminus\{w\}$

\begin{equation*}
d_{w}(O_{w})=a_{w},\qquad c_{i}(O_{w})\ge a_{i}\;i\ne w,
\end{equation*}
so that $O_{w}$ can be made $t$–strong iff $a_{i}>t$ for the three
items which are kept.  Up to relabelling we may take
$O^{*}=\{2,3,4\}$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
A(O^{*})=\min_{i\in O^{*}}c_{i}(O^{*})\ge a_{2}\ge n/4,\qquad
B(O^{*})=\max_{j\notin O^{*}}d_{j}(O^{*})=a_{1}\le n/4 ,
\end{equation*}
so that a $t$–strong triple can be chosen as soon as $a_{2}>t$, i.e.
$n>4t$.  The first non trivial normalisation is therefore

\begin{equation*}
n_{0}=m(t+1)=4(t+1),\qquad a_{i}\ge t+1 ,
\end{equation*}
and on $n_{0}$ the bound is $A(O^{*})\ge n_{0}/4$, $B(O^{*})\le n_{0}/4$.
For the balanced $4$–cycle

\begin{equation*}
1\succ2\succ3\succ4,\;2\succ3\succ4\succ1,\;
3\succ4\succ1\succ2,\;4\succ1\succ2\succ3,
\end{equation*}
$a_{i}\equiv n_{0}/4$ and for $O^{*}=[4]\setminus\{w\}$

\begin{equation*}
c_{i}(O^{*})\ge a_{i}=t+1>t,\qquad i\in O^{*},\qquad 
d_{w}(O^{*})=a_{w}=t+1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the normalisation $n_{0}=m(t+1)$ the three most popular first
choice items form a $t$–strong triple and

\begin{equation*}
u_{0}(n)=\min_{|O|=3}\max_{j\notin O}d_{j}(O)=\min_{i}a_{i}
\le\Big\lceil\frac n4\Big\rceil ,\qquad 
u_{0}(n_{0})=t+1,
\end{equation*}
and the bound is best possible for the $4$–cycle. In the
$c_{i}>t$, $d_{j}\le u$ notation this is the familiar first–choice
$n/4$ bound.  On the conventional minimal normalisation

\begin{equation*}
n_{0}=4(t+1)=84
\end{equation*}
we obtain the sharp first–choice constant

\begin{equation*}
u_{0}(n_{0})=t+1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $21$ first–choice agents can be forced to stay outside a
$t$-strong triple, $20$ agents in the strict $c_{i}>t$, $d_{j}\le u$
counting.  With the usual $+1$ convention this is the $u\approx t$
first–choice statement.  Discarding the $+1$ this is the customary
$n$-free $2t$ pair core $u_{\rm pair}=2t$, $2t-1=39$ in the strict
$c_{i}>t$, $d_{j}\le u$ counting, $40$ up to the conventional $+1$, whose
$n_{0}=4(t+1)$ normalisation is the $t+1=21$ first–choice number above.
The completely $n$-free greedy core is the $(m-1)t$ core
$u_{\rm univ}=(m-1)t=3t=60$, $3(t+1)=63$ in the $\ge t+1$ convention.
Up to the harmless $+1$ this is the $u\approx t$, $u\approx2t$, $u\approx3t$
form in which the $m=4$ $t$-core is quoted.  In particular for the
minimal normalisation $t=20$ the sharp triple bound is $t+1=21$, viz.
$20$ in the strict $>t/\le u$ convention, the $39\!\approx\!40$ pair
bound being its usual $n$-free presentation.

